<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mithridates VI of Pontus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">135–<a href="/years/63-bce">63 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Mithridates the Great, Mithridates VI Eupator</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mithridates VI Eupator was king of Pontus from about 120 BCE and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s most persistent enemy in the east. He built an empire around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, taking the Bosporan Kingdom and Lesser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, and in 88 BCE ordered the massacre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in Asia. He fought three wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a>, Lucullus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>, and died in 63 BCE after his final defeat.</p></div>
